= Research: Toward a predictive science of deep learning

My goal is to uncover the principles governing deep learning and use them to explain mechanisms, make quantitative predictions, and inform algorithm design. 

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="research-details" id="research-perspective">
<summary>Research perspective: scales and effective theories</summary>
<div class="research-details-body">
~~~

My approach draws on three complementary perspectives:

- *Physics.* Finding the right level of description: deciding which details matter at a given scale and building toy models to isolate the underlying mechanisms.

- *Mathematics.* Establishing rigorous results under explicit conditions and clarifying the scope of a theory.

- *Engineering.* Stress-testing theory in real-world systems and refining it through discrepancies between predictions and observations.

Inspired by physics, I view *scale as an organizing principle for deep learning*. I do not expect a single theory to capture learning across all scales; different scales require different [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effective_theory effective theories].

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="research-figure">
<img src="./doc/physics-dl.svg" alt="Different scales, different effective theories in physics and deep learning" style="display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 1000px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto;" />
</div>
~~~

This perspective suggests two complementary routes toward understanding deep learning:

- *Bottom-up.* Starting from simple models and microscopic mechanisms and working toward more complex learning systems.
- *Top-down.* Starting from macroscopic laws and using them to guide the study of underlying mechanisms and intermediate-scale dynamics.

Much of my earlier work followed the bottom-up route, but connecting simplified models to real-world training often proved difficult. Different assumptions and regimes can lead to very different conclusions, without revealing which mechanisms matter in practice. I am now exploring the top-down route: using macroscopic regularities to guide the construction and analysis of microscopic models, so that the two approaches can inform one another.

~~~
{}{raw}
</div>
</details>
~~~

== Functional scaling laws: toward an effective theory of LLM pretraining

We introduced functional scaling laws (FSL): explicit functionals mapping learning-rate and batch-size schedules to loss trajectories. Schematically,

\(
  \underbrace{\mathcal{L}_t}_{\text{loss dynamics}}
  \approx \mathcal{F}_t\!\left[
  \underbrace{\eta(\cdot)}_{\text{learning-rate schedule}},\,
  \underbrace{B(\cdot)}_{\text{batch-size schedule}}
  \right].
\)

Derived in power-law kernel regression, FSL reveals how signal learning and noise forgetting jointly shape learning dynamics. We use these mechanisms to predict and steer LLM pretraining.

- *Functional scaling laws.* We developed the FSL framework and used it to characterize optimal learning-rate and batch-size schedules:
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19189 NeurIPS 2025], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14208 ICLR 2026], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06797 COLT 2026].

- *ELR collapse.* We systematically show that the loss dynamics of LLM pretraining are governed primarily by the effective learning rate (ELR), the ratio of learning rate to parameter norm, rather than the nominal learning rate:
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24814 arXiv 2026].

- *Optimizer mechanisms and design.* We study how optimizer design shapes training dynamics and efficiency, with recent work on momentum [https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02728 arXiv 2026] and blockwise sharpness [https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19002 ICML 2025].

For an overview, see my [./doc/LLM-pretraining-theory.pdf talk slides].

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="research-details" id="fsl-context">
<summary>Broader research context</summary>
<div class="research-details-body">
~~~

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="research-focus-grid">
<div class="research-focus-text">
~~~

[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model Large language model (LLM)] pretraining optimizes a next-token prediction objective at an enormous scale:

\(
  \min_{\theta} \mathcal{R}(\theta),\qquad
  \mathcal{R}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{D}}
  \left[-\sum_{k=2}^{L}\log P_{\theta}(x_k\mid x_1,\dots,x_{k-1})\right].
\)

Here, $P_\theta$ is a Transformer language model, and $\mathcal{D}$ is an internet-scale text corpus. Despite the complexity of this process, pretraining exhibits simple macroscopic regularities, such as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_scaling_law scaling laws]. I study these regularities at three connected levels:

- *Macroscopic laws.* Identify the variables that capture training progress and the quantitative laws relating them across scales.

- *Microscopic mechanisms.* Understand how these laws emerge from high-dimensional learning dynamics.

- *Training principles.* Use these insights to guide optimizer and architecture design, as well as hyperparameter selection, as training scales up.

~~~
